## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming algorithms with bounded memory and error guarantees. Reservoir sampling is a fundamental technique that demonstrates trade-offs between memory, accuracy, and per-element computational cost.

## Key Concepts
- **Reservoir Sampling**: Maintains a uniform random sample of size S from an infinite stream
- **Unbiased Estimator**: (N/S) * sumS has expectation equal to the true sum
- **Relative Error Bound**: Using Chernoff bounds, P(|X - E[X]| > epsilon * E[X]) <= 2 * exp(-S * epsilon^2 / 3), giving S >= (3/epsilon^2) * ln(2/delta)
- **Time Complexity**: O(1) per element (constant-time random number generation and potential replacement)
- **Space Complexity**: O(S) for storing the reservoir

## Derivation
The probability that any given stream element ends up in the final reservoir is S/N. By linearity of expectation, E[sumS] = S/N * (sum of all N values), so E[(N/S) * sumS] = true sum — the estimator is unbiased. The variance of sumS scales as O(N * M^2 / S), and the relative standard deviation is O(sqrt(N/S)), giving the error bounds above.

## Code Example
```javascript
let S = 100;
let N = 0, sumS = 0;
let sample = [];

function process(value) {
  N++;
  if (sample.length < S) {
    sample.push(value);
    sumS += value;
  } else {
    let j = Math.floor(Math.random() * N);
    if (j < S) {
      sumS += value - sample[j];
      sample[j] = value;
    }
  }
}

function estimateSum() {
  return N === 0 ? 0 : (N / S) * sumS;
}
```

## Worked Example
M=100, B=10, S=50. Stream: [5, 12, 7, 23, 8, ...]. After first batch of 5 values (N=5): reservoir = [5,12,7,23,8], sumS = 55, estimate = (5/5)*55 = 55 (exact). After 100 values with sum=2340: reservoir has 50 values summing to ~1170, estimate = (100/50)*1170 = 2340 (close to true). With S=50 and epsilon=0.1, delta ≈ exp(-50*0.01/3) ≈ 0.85 — increase S to 400 for delta=0.01.

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to combine streaming sketches with efficient top-K data structures while reasoning about accuracy, space, and time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- **Streaming Algorithms:** Processing unbounded data with limited memory
- **Count-Min Sketch (CMS):** Probabilistic data structure for frequency estimation with false positives but no false negatives
- **Top-K Maintenance:** Using a min-heap to track the K most frequent elements efficiently
- **Amortized Analysis:** Bounding average cost over sequences of operations
- **Space-Time Trade-offs:** Comparing approximate vs. exact approaches

## Code Example
```javascript
class CountMinSketch {
  constructor(w, d) {
    this.w = w;
    this.d = d;
    this.table = Array.from({ length: d }, () => Array(w).fill(0));
  }

  hash(key, i) {
    const str = typeof key === 'string' ? key : String(key);
    let hash = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < str.length; j++) {
      hash = (hash * 31 + str.charCodeAt(j)) >>> 0;
    }
    return (hash + i * 31) % this.w;
  }

  update(key) {
    for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) {
      const j = this.hash(key, i);
      this.table[i][j]++;
    }
  }

  estimate(key) {
    let min = Infinity;
    for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) {
      const j = this.hash(key, i);
      min = Math.min(min, this.table[i][j]);
    }
    return min;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Designing dynamic SSSP algorithms is essential for real-time network applications. This question tests understanding of worst-case versus amortized guarantees, appropriate data structure selection, and practical implementation constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic shortest paths with nonnegative edge weights
- Localized re-relaxation and bounded Dijkstra execution
- Amortized analysis and worst-case performance bounds
- Memory-efficient data structures and practical optimizations
- Hierarchical update strategies for different edge types

## Code Example
```javascript
// Dynamic SSSP maintenance implementation
class DynamicSSSP {
  constructor(graph, source) {
    this.graph = graph;
    this.source = source;
    this.distances = new Array(graph.N).fill(Infinity);
    this.parents = new Array(graph.N).fill(-1);
    this.heap = new FibonacciHeap();
    this.affectedNodes = new Set();

    // Initialize with Dijkstra
    this.initializeSSSP();
  }

  updateEdge(u, v, newWeight) {
    const oldWeight = this.graph.getWeight(u, v);
    this.graph.setWeight(u, v, newWeight);

    if (newWeight < oldWeight) {
      this.handleWeightDecrease(u, v, newWeight);
    } else {
      this.handleWeightIncrease(u, v, newWeight);
    }
  }

  handleWeightDecrease(u, v, newWeight) {
    if (this.parents[v] === u) {
      // Tree edge weight decreased
      this.propagateDecrease(v);
    } else if (this.distances[u] + newWeight < this.distances[v]) {
      // Non-tree edge creates shorter path
      this.updateDistance(v, this.distances[u] + newWeight, u);
    }
  }

  handleWeightIncrease(u, v, newWeight) {
    if (this.parents[v] === u) {
      // Tree edge weight increased - need re-relaxation
      this.affectedNodes.add(v);
      this.boundedDijkstra([v]);
    }
    // Non-tree edge weight increase requires no action
  }

  boundedDijkstra(startNodes) {
    const queue = new FibonacciHeap();

    for (const node of startNodes) {
      queue.insert(node, this.distances[node]);
    }

    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
      const { node: u, distance: du } = queue.extractMin();

      if (du > this.distances[u]) continue;

      for (const [v, weight] of this.graph.neighbors(u)) {
        const newDist = du + weight;
        if (newDist < this.distances[v]) {
          this.updateDistance(v, newDist, u);
          queue.insert(v, newDist);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  updateDistance(node, newDistance, parent) {
    this.distances[node] = newDistance;
    this.parents[node] = parent;
    this.affectedNodes.add(node);
  }

  propagateDecrease(startNode) {
    const queue = new FibonacciHeap();
    queue.insert(startNode, this.distances[startNode]);

    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
      const { node: u } = queue.extractMin();

      for (const [v, weight] of this.graph.neighbors(u)) {
        if (this.parents[v] === u) {
          const newDist = this.distances[u] + weight;
          if (newDist < this.distances[v]) {
            this.updateDistance(v, newDist, u);
            queue.insert(v, newDist);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  initializeSSSP() {
    // Standard Dijkstra implementation
    this.distances[this.source] = 0;
    const heap = new FibonacciHeap();
    heap.insert(this.source, 0);

    while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
      const { node: u, distance: du } = heap.extractMin();

      if (du > this.distances[u]) continue;

      for (const [v, weight] of this.graph.neighbors(u)) {
        const newDist = du + weight;
        if (newDist < this.distances[v]) {
          this.distances[v] = newDist;
          this.parents[v] = u;
          heap.insert(v, newDist);
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

This implementation demonstrates the core principles of dynamic SSSP maintenance with efficient localized updates and amortized performance guarantees.

